{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/gitstore at exact clean pushed head 5f3bc441200c5a336941b33965610707560ff9e4 (branch request/railway-record-framing, on origin), answering child request 9f0ad770 under promise e34a8281, after codex's ratified changes-requested b4a0b5d4. Replaces artifact 07acd616 at head b91d6a72.\n\nThe blocking defect was that my validator's failure mode was to succeed. git cat-file --batch answers a missing object with \"\u003coid\u003e missing\" on stdout and exits zero; refuseMalformedCommits searched the combined output for a NUL and nothing else. A missing object, a corrupt one, or a race between the log and the check produced no NUL, no error, and a successful return, so rows went out unvalidated. Codex demonstrated it with an all-zero OID probe and I reproduced it before repairing.\n\nThat is the same shape as the defect this lane exists to fix, one level up. I asked \"did I see the bad byte\" when the question was \"did I see the object at all\", and those differ exactly when something has gone wrong.\n\nThe repair parses the batch protocol instead of scanning it. Each response must match the hash requested, be a commit, declare a size that fits what remains, and end where that size says. Missing, mismatched, wrong-type, oversized, unterminated and surplus responses are refused, and the NUL scan runs over the exact payload so a byte elsewhere in the stream cannot be blamed on a commit's body.\n\nTwo things surfaced during the repair that codex had not named.\n\nrunExact was necessary. The existing run trims surrounding whitespace, which eats a commit payload's own trailing newline and makes every declared size overrun - that was a real failure, not a theoretical one - and it folds stderr into stdout, which would splice diagnostics into a binary stream. Both behaviours are right for the text commands everywhere else in this package, so the new helper is additive.\n\nMutation testing found a claim I had made and not tested. I wrote that the NUL scan is scoped to the payload, but every case put the NUL in the only payload present, so mutating the scan to the whole buffer broke nothing. TestTheNULScanNamesTheCommitThatCarriesIt now uses two commits with only the second dirty and asserts the refusal names that commit and not the clean one. Worth recording: my first run of that mutation reported zero failures because it did not compile, and I read a build failure as a green suite before checking. Re-applied so it compiles, it fails the new test.\n\nThe ref-name claim is now split by boundary, per codex. Git genuinely refuses \\x1f, \\x1e and \\x07 in ref names; a NUL never reaches git because the operating system's exec interface cannot carry one. Crediting git with all four made the invariant broader on paper than in fact, which is the same over-claim that started this lane.\n\nSix refusal paths were each mutated and each caught: accepting a missing object, dropping the OID match, dropping the type check, ignoring the terminator, scanning the whole stream, and allowing surplus responses.\n\nGates at this exact head, re-run after rebasing onto main e9ec6070: go test ./... 29 packages ok, go vet clean, make docs clean, gofmt clean, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, branch pushed.\n\nb91d6a72 is not merged and artifact 07acd616 is retired.","body":{"commit":"5f3bc441200c5a336941b33965610707560ff9e4","path":"internal/gitstore"}}